 ],
 "constraints": [
  "gcd(p, q) == 1"
 ],
 "stem": "If $\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{{=p}}{{=q}}$, find the value of $\\frac{{=A}x^2+{=B}xy}{{=C}y^2+{=D}xy}$ as a fraction in its simplest form.",
 "answer": "{=rn}/{=rd}",
 "solution": [
  "Since $\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{{=p}}{{=q}}$, let $x={=p}m$ and $y={=q}m$ with $m\\neq 0$.",
  "Numerator: ${=A}x^2+{=B}xy={=A}\\times {=p*p}m^2+{=B}\\times {=p*q}m^2={=nn}m^2$.",
  "Denominator: ${=C}y^2+{=D}xy={=C}\\times {=q*q}m^2+{=D}\\times {=p*q}m^2={=dd}m^2$.",
  "$\\frac{{=nn}m^2}{{=dd}m^2}=\\frac{{=nn}}{{=dd}}=\\frac{{=rn}}{{=rd}}$ in simplest form.",
  "The value of the ratio is $\\frac{{=rn}}{{=rd}}$."
 ],
 "marker": {
  "kind": "expression",
  "answer": "{=rn}/{=rd}",
  "form": "simplest",
  "variables": [],
  "tolerance": null
 },
 "proposed_misconceptions": [],
 "notes": "Extends the book's substitution x=pm, y=qm to degree-2 homogeneous expressions. Reduced fraction is computed; rd != 1 required."
}
